    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Brent-LPG Correlation</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">r = ''' + str(d['temporal']['correlation_lpg']) + '''</div>
      <div class="kpi-note">Pearson coefficient across all country-weeks</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The overall Brent-to-retail correlation of <span class="highlight">r = ''' + str(d['temporal']['correlation_petrol']) + '''</span> appears moderate at the global level. This is because the aggregate correlation pools together countries with vastly different subsidy and tax regimes: subsidized economies dampen the Brent signal, pulling the aggregate correlation down. As shown in Section 4, the correlation rises to r = ''' + str(d['subsidy'].get('corr_Low_petrol', 'N/A')) + ''' for Low-subsidy countries and falls to r = ''' + str(d['subsidy'].get('corr_Very High_petrol', 'N/A')) + ''' for Very High-subsidy countries.</p>

