<!-- SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS & POLICY IMPLICATIONS -->
<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 1: Subsidies are the dominant price determinant, not income level or geography.</strong><br>
    The subsidy dampening effect of 79.5% (High vs. Low) and 95.7% (Very High vs. Low) far exceeds the impact of any other variable. Countries at identical income levels with different subsidy regimes show 3-4x price differences. <em>Policy implication:</em> Subsidy design is the most powerful lever available to policymakers for controlling consumer fuel prices, but its fiscal sustainability must be weighed against opportunity costs in public spending.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 2: Brent Crude pass-through varies 23x between subsidy extremes.</strong><br>
    Low-subsidy countries transmit $0.032 of retail price change per $1 Brent movement, while Very High-subsidy countries transmit only $0.001. <em>Policy implication:</em> Countries seeking to insulate consumers from global volatility must accept near-total absorption of commodity risk in the national budget. Partial subsidies (Medium level, $0.016/L per $1/bbl) offer a middle ground but still leave consumers exposed to roughly half the crude price signal.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 3: Low-income countries face the highest price volatility despite lower absolute prices.</strong><br>
    The coefficient of variation for petrol prices in low-income countries (0.644) is 52% higher than in high-income countries (0.424). <em>Policy implication:</em> Price stabilization mechanisms (strategic reserves, smoothing funds, targeted cash transfers) are most urgently needed in low-income settings, where consumers have the fewest resources to absorb shocks and governments have the least fiscal space for subsidies.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 4: The COVID-19 demand shock produced asymmetric price pass-through.</strong><br>
    When Brent fell 29.5% from pre-COVID levels, retail petrol dropped only 29.7% globally, but this average masks wide variation: Low-subsidy countries absorbed most of the decline, while High/Very High-subsidy countries barely moved, as prices were already near subsidy floors. <em>Policy implication:</em> Price floors inherent in subsidy regimes prevent consumers from benefiting from commodity downturns, creating a ratchet effect where prices rise with crude but rarely fall proportionally.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 5: Regional price inequality is widening.</strong><br>
    The Europe-Middle East spread grew from $1.61/L (2020) to $2.87/L (2026), a 78% increase in global pricing inequality. This divergence is driven by Europe's rising environmental taxes and the Middle East's maintained subsidy floors. <em>Policy implication:</em> As high-income nations increasingly use fuel taxation as a climate policy tool, the gap with subsidized economies will continue to widen, creating fundamentally different energy cost structures that affect industrial competitiveness and consumer welfare.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 6: Tax-to-price ratios vary systematically with income, creating an equity paradox.</strong><br>
    High-income countries levy 34.7% tax on fuel (a progressive model where wealthier populations fund public goods), while middle-income countries levy only 21.1% and low-income countries 22.9%. However, since low-income countries already face higher price volatility, the lower tax rate provides limited relief. <em>Policy implication:</em> Tax calibration should account for volatility exposure, not just absolute price levels. Low-income countries could explore counter-cyclical tax adjustments that reduce rates during price spikes and increase them during troughs.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 7: The 2022 energy crisis validated subsidy insulation but at high fiscal cost.</strong><br>
    During the 2022 crisis, Very High-subsidy countries saw petrol increase only $0.018/L from 2021 to 2022 (+12.2%), while Low-subsidy countries saw a $0.341/L increase (+9.9%). However, maintaining these subsidies during $130/bbl Brent required enormous fiscal transfers. <em>Policy implication:</em> Targeted subsidies (e.g., for essential transport and low-income households) rather than blanket price controls can achieve consumer protection at lower fiscal cost, while preserving price signals that encourage efficiency.
  </div>

  <h3>Recommendations for Policymakers</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Move from blanket subsidies to targeted transfers.</strong> Universal fuel subsidies benefit high-consumption households disproportionately. Direct cash transfers or transport vouchers for vulnerable populations achieve consumer protection at 30-50% lower fiscal cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Establish automatic stabilization mechanisms.</strong> Smoothing funds that accumulate tax revenue during low-price periods and release it during spikes can buffer volatility without permanent fiscal distortion. Chile's Fuel Price Stabilization Mechanism (MEPCO) offers a proven model.</li>
    <li><strong>Use counter-cyclical tax adjustments.</strong> Reducing fuel taxes during price spikes and restoring them during troughs can stabilize consumer costs while preserving long-term revenue. This requires credible policy frameworks to prevent "temporary" cuts from becoming permanent.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize strategic petroleum reserves in low-income countries.</strong> Physical reserves provide a 60-90 day buffer against supply disruptions at lower fiscal cost than permanent subsidies. Regional reserve-sharing agreements can spread the investment burden.</li>
    <li><strong>Coordinate climate taxation with social policy.</strong> As high-income nations increase fuel taxes to meet climate targets, earmarking a share of revenue for energy affordability programs can prevent regressive outcomes and maintain public support for the transition.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

